---
title: Data and Channels
description: Map typed application data to positions, grouping, color, radius, and stable chart identity.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Marks consume ordinary iterables. Channels say which values from those rows control position, grouping, color, size, or identity."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Charts does not require a universal series shape. Keep the data model that best represents the problem, and let each mark consume the rows it needs."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"field-channels","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Field channels"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a field name when the value already exists:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"lineY(rows, {\n  x: 'date',\n  y: 'revenue',\n  z: 'region',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The field list is type-filtered. For example:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A numeric "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"barX"},{"type":"text","value":" length accepts numeric fields."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A date-based "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lineY"},{"type":"text","value":" x channel accepts a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Date"},{"type":"text","value":" field."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts string or number fields."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nullable positional fields are valid when the mark defines missing-value behavior."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If TypeScript rejects a field name, do not cast it. Correct the row type, choose the intended field, or use a typed accessor."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"accessor-channels","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessor channels"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use an accessor for derived values:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"dot(rows, {\n  x: (row) => row.revenue / row.accounts,\n  y: (row) => row.retained / row.accounts,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every accessor receives:"}]},{"type":"code","value":";(datum, { index, data }) => value","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"datum"},{"type":"text","value":" has the exact source type. The context contains the zero-based "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"index"},{"type":"text","value":"\nand readonly materialized "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" array. Accessors are evaluated when the mark\ninitializes; keep expensive cross-row transforms in application code."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"positional-channels","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Positional channels"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The x and y channels feed the reserved scales with the same names:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"barX(rows, {\n  x: 'revenue',\n  y: 'region',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xScale"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"yScale"},{"type":"text","value":" when a mark should feed another named entry in the\nchart's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scales"},{"type":"text","value":" registry. Channel names continue to describe geometry; scale\nIDs select the mapping."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Positional values are also retained in each interaction "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartPoint"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const handleFocus = (point: ChartPoint<Row, number, string> | null) => {\n  if (!point) return\n  console.log(point.datum, point.xValue, point.yValue)\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Normal callbacks infer these types from the definition, so explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartPoint"},{"type":"text","value":" annotations are usually unnecessary."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"string"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Date"},{"type":"text","value":" are the supported chart value types. A definition can infer a union when conditional branches intentionally use different coordinate types; narrow that union with normal TypeScript control flow."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"grouping-with-z","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouping with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" identifies a semantic series or group:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"lineY(rows, {\n  x: 'date',\n  y: 'value',\n  z: 'region',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For connected line and area marks, an explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" partitions observations\ninto independent geometry. If "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" is omitted and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" is authored, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":"\nalso supplies that path grouping. When both are present, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" wins for geometry\nand interaction grouping while "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" remains an independent color-scale\nvalue. Omitting "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" reuses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" for color."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bars stack their length channel by default. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"layout: group()"},{"type":"text","value":" when multiple\nbars must occupy sub-bands within one category. Grouping uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" when present,\notherwise a discrete "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" channel may infer series identity. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../examples/bars-and-rankings.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bars and Rankings"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"color-channels-and-constants","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Color channels and constants"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are two common paths:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" is a semantic mapping resolved by the chart color scale."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" falls back into that mapping when no separate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" channel is set."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fill"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stroke"},{"type":"text","value":" are final paint overrides and bypass scale mapping for\nthat paint."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The default categorical palette is useful for quick distinctions. Use an\nexplicit ordinal scale when a category must always map to the same color across\ncharts, filters, and sessions."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'\n\nconst segmentColor = scaleOrdinal(\n  ['Consumer', 'Enterprise', 'Public'],\n  ['#2563eb', '#f97316', '#10b981'],\n)\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    dot(rows, {\n      x: 'revenue',\n      y: 'retention',\n      z: 'segment',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: revenueScale },\n    y: { scale: retentionScale },\n  },\n\n  color: {\n    scale: segmentColor,\n    legend: colorLegend({ label: 'Segment' }),\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The lightweight ordinal scale owns the stable category mapping. "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/legends-and-color.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Legends and\nColor"}]},{"type":"text","value":" covers continuous color, gradients, and\napplication-wide palettes."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"radius-is-explicit","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Radius is explicit"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"r"},{"type":"text","value":" option on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dot"},{"type":"text","value":" is a pixel radius unless "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rScale"},{"type":"text","value":" is supplied:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"dot(rows, {\n  x: 'revenue',\n  y: 'retention',\n  r: 'accounts',\n  rScale: {\n    scale: () => scaleSqrt().range([3, 22]),\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This direct "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleSqrt"},{"type":"text","value":" import belongs to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":" and requires the matching direct dependency and type package."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keeping the scale visible makes the perceptual encoding reviewable. It also avoids silently treating a business measure as pixels."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"stable-identity","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable identity"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Built-in marks infer identity in this order:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A unique string or number "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"datum.id"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A unique string or number "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"datum.data.id"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A unique mark-specific positional identity"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row index"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bars use their categorical channel. Lines and areas use their independent\naxis. Dots and text try x, then y, then the x/y tuple. Rects and cells use\ntheir x/y interval tuple. These candidates are checked within each interaction\ngroup; a collision rejects the candidate and continues to the next fallback.\nThe nested ID convention covers rows emitted by wrappers such as D3 pie\nwithout requiring an accessor solely to unwrap "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data.id"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For common rows with a unique "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":", no key option is required:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"barX(rows, {\n  id: 'product-ranking',\n  x: 'value',\n  y: 'product',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use an explicit key when identity lives in another field, the inferred\npositional value can change, or the automatic candidates are not unique:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"barX(rows, {\n  x: 'value',\n  y: 'product',\n  key: 'productId',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explicit keys are string or number values and need to be unique within the\nmark and group. When a mark-owned positional candidate is present but\nincomplete or duplicated, the mark falls back to array position and warns once\nper mark instance in development. Marks with no positional candidate also use\nrow position after checking IDs, without adding a warning for ordinary static\ndata."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The mark "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":" identifies the layer. Give conditionally rendered or reordered marks an explicit, stable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":" as well."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"missing-and-invalid-values","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Missing and invalid values"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Marks ignore positional observations they cannot materialize."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lineY"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"areaY"},{"type":"text","value":" split geometry at missing or non-finite positions."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dot"},{"type":"text","value":", bars, rectangles, rules, and text omit invalid observations."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A negative dot radius is invalid."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A null group means “ungrouped.”"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Model a genuinely missing observation as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" in the field type. Do not replace it with zero unless zero is the correct domain value."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For lines, a gap communicates missing data:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface Reading {\n  id: string\n  time: Date\n  temperature: number | null\n}\n\nlineY(readings, {\n  x: 'time',\n  y: 'temperature',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"different-marks-can-use-different-rows","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Different marks can use different rows"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Layering does not force one datum union:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const marks = [\n  rect(maintenanceWindows, {\n    x1: 'start',\n    x2: 'end',\n    y1: 'minimum',\n    y2: 'maximum',\n  }),\n  lineY(readings, {\n    x: 'time',\n    y: 'temperature',\n  }),\n  text(annotations, {\n    x: 'time',\n    y: 'value',\n    text: 'label',\n  }),\n]","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The definition’s interaction datum becomes the honest union of point-emitting mark data. Callbacks narrow that union using your existing discriminants or type guards."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"derived-data-stays-explicit","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Derived data stays explicit"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouping, binning, rolling, normalization, selection, and reusable stack\nendpoints happen before mark construction. Use the pure transforms from\nTanStack Charts or an ordinary application function:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const bins = binX(observations, {\n  value: 'latency',\n  thresholds: 24,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transforms return materialized typed rows and retain source lineage. They do\nnot rewrite mark options or own reactivity. Run them beside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineChart"},{"type":"text","value":" or\ninside the framework primitive that memoizes the definition. The resulting\nrows flow into ordinary marks."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../guides/transforms-and-reactivity.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transforms and Reactivity"}]},{"type":"text","value":" shows the\ncomplete raw-data-to-mark path and separates application memoization from\nresponsive layout work."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"complete-bubble-scatter-example","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete bubble-scatter example"}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"bubble-scatter-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"bubble-scatter","data-collapsed-indexes":"[1]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { scaleSqrt } from 'd3-scale'\\nimport { colorLegend, defineChart, dot } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\\nimport { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'\\nimport { penguins, type PenguinsRow } from './data'\\n\\ntype CompletePenguin = PenguinsRow & {\\n  culmen_length_mm: number\\n  culmen_depth_mm: number\\n  body_mass_g: number\\n}\\n\\nconst rows = penguins.filter(\\n  (row): row is CompletePenguin =>\\n    row.culmen_length_mm !== null &&\\n    row.culmen_depth_mm !== null &&\\n    row.body_mass_g !== null,\\n)\\nconst species = ['Adelie', 'Chinstrap', 'Gentoo']\\n\\nexport default defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    dot(rows, {\\n      x: 'culmen_length_mm',\\n      y: 'culmen_depth_mm',\\n      color: 'species',\\n      r: 'body_mass_g',\\n      rScale: {\\n        scale: () => scaleSqrt().range([3, 11]),\\n      },\\n      fillOpacity: 0.78,\\n      stroke: 'currentColor',\\n      strokeOpacity: 0.28,\\n      strokeWidth: 0.75,\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: {\\n      scale: scaleLinear,\\n      grid: true,\\n      axis: { label: 'Bill length (mm)' },\\n    },\\n    y: {\\n      scale: scaleLinear,\\n      grid: true,\\n      axis: { label: 'Bill depth (mm)' },\\n    },\\n  },\\n\\n  color: {\\n    scale: scaleOrdinal(species, ['#2563eb', '#f97316', '#10b981']),\\n    legend: colorLegend({ label: 'Species' }),\\n  },\\n})\",\"/src/data.ts\":\"export interface PenguinsRow {\\n  species: string\\n  culmen_length_mm: number | null\\n  culmen_depth_mm: number | null\\n  body_mass_g: number | null\\n}\\n\\nexport const penguins: readonly PenguinsRow[] = [\\n  {\\n    species: 'Adelie',\\n    culmen_length_mm: 39.1,\\n    culmen_depth_mm: 18.7,\\n    body_mass_g: 3750,\\n  },\\n  {\\n    species: 'Adelie',\\n    culmen_length_mm: 40.3,\\n    culmen_depth_mm: 18,\\n    body_mass_g: 3250,\\n  },\\n  {\\n    species: 'Chinstrap',\\n    culmen_length_mm: 46.5,\\n    culmen_depth_mm: 17.9,\\n    body_mass_g: 3500,\\n  },\\n  {\\n    species: 'Chinstrap',\\n    culmen_length_mm: 50,\\n    culmen_depth_mm: 19.5,\\n    body_mass_g: 3900,\\n  },\\n  {\\n    species: 'Gentoo',\\n    culmen_length_mm: 46.1,\\n    culmen_depth_mm: 13.2,\\n    body_mass_g: 4500,\\n  },\\n  {\\n    species: 'Gentoo',\\n    culmen_length_mm: 50,\\n    culmen_depth_mm: 16.3,\\n    body_mass_g: 5700,\\n  },\\n  {\\n    species: 'Gentoo',\\n    culmen_length_mm: null,\\n    culmen_depth_mm: null,\\n    body_mass_g: null,\\n  },\\n]\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleSqrt } from 'd3-scale'\nimport { colorLegend, defineChart, dot } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'\nimport { penguins, type PenguinsRow } from './data'\n\ntype CompletePenguin = PenguinsRow & {\n  culmen_length_mm: number\n  culmen_depth_mm: number\n  body_mass_g: number\n}\n\nconst rows = penguins.filter(\n  (row): row is CompletePenguin =>\n    row.culmen_length_mm !== null &&\n    row.culmen_depth_mm !== null &&\n    row.body_mass_g !== null,\n)\nconst species = ['Adelie', 'Chinstrap', 'Gentoo']\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    dot(rows, {\n      x: 'culmen_length_mm',\n      y: 'culmen_depth_mm',\n      color: 'species',\n      r: 'body_mass_g',\n      rScale: {\n        scale: () => scaleSqrt().range([3, 11]),\n      },\n      fillOpacity: 0.78,\n      stroke: 'currentColor',\n      strokeOpacity: 0.28,\n      strokeWidth: 0.75,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Bill length (mm)' },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Bill depth (mm)' },\n    },\n  },\n\n  color: {\n    scale: scaleOrdinal(species, ['#2563eb', '#f97316', '#10b981']),\n    legend: colorLegend({ label: 'Species' }),\n  },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=bubble-scatter env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"},{"type":"code","value":"export interface PenguinsRow {\n  species: string\n  culmen_length_mm: number | null\n  culmen_depth_mm: number | null\n  body_mass_g: number | null\n}\n\nexport const penguins: readonly PenguinsRow[] = [\n  {\n    species: 'Adelie',\n    culmen_length_mm: 39.1,\n    culmen_depth_mm: 18.7,\n    body_mass_g: 3750,\n  },\n  {\n    species: 'Adelie',\n    culmen_length_mm: 40.3,\n    culmen_depth_mm: 18,\n    body_mass_g: 3250,\n  },\n  {\n    species: 'Chinstrap',\n    culmen_length_mm: 46.5,\n    culmen_depth_mm: 17.9,\n    body_mass_g: 3500,\n  },\n  {\n    species: 'Chinstrap',\n    culmen_length_mm: 50,\n    culmen_depth_mm: 19.5,\n    body_mass_g: 3900,\n  },\n  {\n    species: 'Gentoo',\n    culmen_length_mm: 46.1,\n    culmen_depth_mm: 13.2,\n    body_mass_g: 4500,\n  },\n  {\n    species: 'Gentoo',\n    culmen_length_mm: 50,\n    culmen_depth_mm: 16.3,\n    body_mass_g: 5700,\n  },\n  {\n    species: 'Gentoo',\n    culmen_length_mm: null,\n    culmen_depth_mm: null,\n    body_mass_g: null,\n  },\n]","lang":"ts","meta":"group=bubble-scatter file=/src/data.ts collapsed","title":"/src/data.ts","file":"/src/data.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The filter only removes observations missing a plotted measurement; the marks\nstill use the source dataset's field names. The axes and categorical color use\nlightweight scales. Bubble area needs the nonlinear D3 "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleSqrt"},{"type":"text","value":", so install\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@types/d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":" for that one mapping."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For every built-in channel, see the relevant "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/line-and-area.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mark Reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":". For inference rules and custom datum unions, see "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/typescript.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TypeScript"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"field-channels","text":"Field channels","level":2},{"id":"accessor-channels","text":"Accessor channels","level":2},{"id":"positional-channels","text":"Positional channels","level":2},{"id":"grouping-with-z","text":"Grouping with z","level":2},{"id":"color-channels-and-constants","text":"Color channels and constants","level":2},{"id":"radius-is-explicit","text":"Radius is explicit","level":2},{"id":"stable-identity","text":"Stable identity","level":2},{"id":"missing-and-invalid-values","text":"Missing and invalid values","level":2},{"id":"different-marks-can-use-different-rows","text":"Different marks can use different rows","level":2},{"id":"derived-data-stays-explicit","text":"Derived data stays explicit","level":2},{"id":"complete-bubble-scatter-example","text":"Complete bubble-scatter example","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nMarks consume ordinary iterables. Channels say which values from those rows control position, grouping, color, size, or identity.\n\nTanStack Charts does not require a universal series shape. Keep the data model that best represents the problem, and let each mark consume the rows it needs.\n\n## Field channels\n\nUse a field name when the value already exists:\n\n```ts\nlineY(rows, {\n  x: 'date',\n  y: 'revenue',\n  z: 'region',\n})\n```\n\nThe field list is type-filtered. For example:\n\n- A numeric `barX` length accepts numeric fields.\n- A date-based `lineY` x channel accepts a `Date` field.\n- `key` accepts string or number fields.\n- Nullable positional fields are valid when the mark defines missing-value behavior.\n\nIf TypeScript rejects a field name, do not cast it. Correct the row type, choose the intended field, or use a typed accessor.\n\n## Accessor channels\n\nUse an accessor for derived values:\n\n```ts\ndot(rows, {\n  x: (row) => row.revenue / row.accounts,\n  y: (row) => row.retained / row.accounts,\n})\n```\n\nEvery accessor receives:\n\n```ts\n;(datum, { index, data }) => value\n```\n\n`datum` has the exact source type. The context contains the zero-based `index`\nand readonly materialized `data` array. Accessors are evaluated when the mark\ninitializes; keep expensive cross-row transforms in application code.\n\n## Positional channels\n\nThe x and y channels feed the reserved scales with the same names:\n\n```ts\nbarX(rows, {\n  x: 'revenue',\n  y: 'region',\n})\n```\n\nUse `xScale` or `yScale` when a mark should feed another named entry in the\nchart's `scales` registry. Channel names continue to describe geometry; scale\nIDs select the mapping.\n\nPositional values are also retained in each interaction `ChartPoint`:\n\n```ts\nconst handleFocus = (point: ChartPoint<Row, number, string> | null) => {\n  if (!point) return\n  console.log(point.datum, point.xValue, point.yValue)\n}\n```\n\nNormal callbacks infer these types from the definition, so explicit `ChartPoint` annotations are usually unnecessary.\n\n`number`, `string`, and `Date` are the supported chart value types. A definition can infer a union when conditional branches intentionally use different coordinate types; narrow that union with normal TypeScript control flow.\n\n## Grouping with `z`\n\n`z` identifies a semantic series or group:\n\n```ts\nlineY(rows, {\n  x: 'date',\n  y: 'value',\n  z: 'region',\n})\n```\n\nFor connected line and area marks, an explicit `z` partitions observations\ninto independent geometry. If `z` is omitted and `color` is authored, `color`\nalso supplies that path grouping. When both are present, `z` wins for geometry\nand interaction grouping while `color` remains an independent color-scale\nvalue. Omitting `color` reuses `z` for color.\n\nBars stack their length channel by default. Use `layout: group()` when multiple\nbars must occupy sub-bands within one category. Grouping uses `z` when present,\notherwise a discrete `color` channel may infer series identity. See\n[Bars and Rankings](../examples/bars-and-rankings.md).\n\n## Color channels and constants\n\nThere are two common paths:\n\n- `color` is a semantic mapping resolved by the chart color scale.\n- `z` falls back into that mapping when no separate `color` channel is set.\n- `fill` and `stroke` are final paint overrides and bypass scale mapping for\n  that paint.\n\nThe default categorical palette is useful for quick distinctions. Use an\nexplicit ordinal scale when a category must always map to the same color across\ncharts, filters, and sessions.\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'\n\nconst segmentColor = scaleOrdinal(\n  ['Consumer', 'Enterprise', 'Public'],\n  ['#2563eb', '#f97316', '#10b981'],\n)\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    dot(rows, {\n      x: 'revenue',\n      y: 'retention',\n      z: 'segment',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: revenueScale },\n    y: { scale: retentionScale },\n  },\n\n  color: {\n    scale: segmentColor,\n    legend: colorLegend({ label: 'Segment' }),\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe lightweight ordinal scale owns the stable category mapping. [Legends and\nColor](../guides/legends-and-color.md) covers continuous color, gradients, and\napplication-wide palettes.\n\n## Radius is explicit\n\nThe `r` option on `dot` is a pixel radius unless `rScale` is supplied:\n\n```ts\ndot(rows, {\n  x: 'revenue',\n  y: 'retention',\n  r: 'accounts',\n  rScale: {\n    scale: () => scaleSqrt().range([3, 22]),\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThis direct `scaleSqrt` import belongs to `d3-scale` and requires the matching direct dependency and type package.\n\nKeeping the scale visible makes the perceptual encoding reviewable. It also avoids silently treating a business measure as pixels.\n\n## Stable identity\n\nBuilt-in marks infer identity in this order:\n\n1. An explicit `key`\n2. A unique string or number `datum.id`\n3. A unique string or number `datum.data.id`\n4. A unique mark-specific positional identity\n5. Row index\n\nBars use their categorical channel. Lines and areas use their independent\naxis. Dots and text try x, then y, then the x/y tuple. Rects and cells use\ntheir x/y interval tuple. These candidates are checked within each interaction\ngroup; a collision rejects the candidate and continues to the next fallback.\nThe nested ID convention covers rows emitted by wrappers such as D3 pie\nwithout requiring an accessor solely to unwrap `data.id`.\n\nFor common rows with a unique `id`, no key option is required:\n\n```ts\nbarX(rows, {\n  id: 'product-ranking',\n  x: 'value',\n  y: 'product',\n})\n```\n\nUse an explicit key when identity lives in another field, the inferred\npositional value can change, or the automatic candidates are not unique:\n\n```ts\nbarX(rows, {\n  x: 'value',\n  y: 'product',\n  key: 'productId',\n})\n```\n\nExplicit keys are string or number values and need to be unique within the\nmark and group. When a mark-owned positional candidate is present but\nincomplete or duplicated, the mark falls back to array position and warns once\nper mark instance in development. Marks with no positional candidate also use\nrow position after checking IDs, without adding a warning for ordinary static\ndata.\n\nThe mark `id` identifies the layer. Give conditionally rendered or reordered marks an explicit, stable `id` as well.\n\n## Missing and invalid values\n\nMarks ignore positional observations they cannot materialize.\n\n- `lineY` and `areaY` split geometry at missing or non-finite positions.\n- `dot`, bars, rectangles, rules, and text omit invalid observations.\n- A negative dot radius is invalid.\n- A null group means “ungrouped.”\n\nModel a genuinely missing observation as `null` or `undefined` in the field type. Do not replace it with zero unless zero is the correct domain value.\n\nFor lines, a gap communicates missing data:\n\n```ts\ninterface Reading {\n  id: string\n  time: Date\n  temperature: number | null\n}\n\nlineY(readings, {\n  x: 'time',\n  y: 'temperature',\n})\n```\n\n## Different marks can use different rows\n\nLayering does not force one datum union:\n\n```ts\nconst marks = [\n  rect(maintenanceWindows, {\n    x1: 'start',\n    x2: 'end',\n    y1: 'minimum',\n    y2: 'maximum',\n  }),\n  lineY(readings, {\n    x: 'time',\n    y: 'temperature',\n  }),\n  text(annotations, {\n    x: 'time',\n    y: 'value',\n    text: 'label',\n  }),\n]\n```\n\nThe definition’s interaction datum becomes the honest union of point-emitting mark data. Callbacks narrow that union using your existing discriminants or type guards.\n\n## Derived data stays explicit\n\nGrouping, binning, rolling, normalization, selection, and reusable stack\nendpoints happen before mark construction. Use the pure transforms from\nTanStack Charts or an ordinary application function:\n\n```ts\nconst bins = binX(observations, {\n  value: 'latency',\n  thresholds: 24,\n})\n```\n\nTransforms return materialized typed rows and retain source lineage. They do\nnot rewrite mark options or own reactivity. Run them beside `defineChart` or\ninside the framework primitive that memoizes the definition. The resulting\nrows flow into ordinary marks.\n\n[Transforms and Reactivity](../guides/transforms-and-reactivity.md) shows the\ncomplete raw-data-to-mark path and separates application memoization from\nresponsive layout work.\n\n## Complete bubble-scatter example\n\n```ts group=bubble-scatter env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { scaleSqrt } from 'd3-scale'\nimport { colorLegend, defineChart, dot } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'\nimport { penguins, type PenguinsRow } from './data'\n\ntype CompletePenguin = PenguinsRow & {\n  culmen_length_mm: number\n  culmen_depth_mm: number\n  body_mass_g: number\n}\n\nconst rows = penguins.filter(\n  (row): row is CompletePenguin =>\n    row.culmen_length_mm !== null &&\n    row.culmen_depth_mm !== null &&\n    row.body_mass_g !== null,\n)\nconst species = ['Adelie', 'Chinstrap', 'Gentoo']\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    dot(rows, {\n      x: 'culmen_length_mm',\n      y: 'culmen_depth_mm',\n      color: 'species',\n      r: 'body_mass_g',\n      rScale: {\n        scale: () => scaleSqrt().range([3, 11]),\n      },\n      fillOpacity: 0.78,\n      stroke: 'currentColor',\n      strokeOpacity: 0.28,\n      strokeWidth: 0.75,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Bill length (mm)' },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Bill depth (mm)' },\n    },\n  },\n\n  color: {\n    scale: scaleOrdinal(species, ['#2563eb', '#f97316', '#10b981']),\n    legend: colorLegend({ label: 'Species' }),\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=bubble-scatter file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport interface PenguinsRow {\n  species: string\n  culmen_length_mm: number | null\n  culmen_depth_mm: number | null\n  body_mass_g: number | null\n}\n\nexport const penguins: readonly PenguinsRow[] = [\n  {\n    species: 'Adelie',\n    culmen_length_mm: 39.1,\n    culmen_depth_mm: 18.7,\n    body_mass_g: 3750,\n  },\n  {\n    species: 'Adelie',\n    culmen_length_mm: 40.3,\n    culmen_depth_mm: 18,\n    body_mass_g: 3250,\n  },\n  {\n    species: 'Chinstrap',\n    culmen_length_mm: 46.5,\n    culmen_depth_mm: 17.9,\n    body_mass_g: 3500,\n  },\n  {\n    species: 'Chinstrap',\n    culmen_length_mm: 50,\n    culmen_depth_mm: 19.5,\n    body_mass_g: 3900,\n  },\n  {\n    species: 'Gentoo',\n    culmen_length_mm: 46.1,\n    culmen_depth_mm: 13.2,\n    body_mass_g: 4500,\n  },\n  {\n    species: 'Gentoo',\n    culmen_length_mm: 50,\n    culmen_depth_mm: 16.3,\n    body_mass_g: 5700,\n  },\n  {\n    species: 'Gentoo',\n    culmen_length_mm: null,\n    culmen_depth_mm: null,\n    body_mass_g: null,\n  },\n]\n```\n\nThe filter only removes observations missing a plotted measurement; the marks\nstill use the source dataset's field names. The axes and categorical color use\nlightweight scales. Bubble area needs the nonlinear D3 `scaleSqrt`, so install\n`d3-scale` and `@types/d3-scale` for that one mapping.\n\nFor every built-in channel, see the relevant [Mark Reference](../reference/marks/line-and-area.md). For inference rules and custom datum unions, see [TypeScript](../guides/typescript.md).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Data and Channels\ndescription: Map typed application data to positions, grouping, color, radius, and stable chart identity.\n---\n\nMarks consume ordinary iterables. Channels say which values from those rows control position, grouping, color, size, or identity.\n\nTanStack Charts does not require a universal series shape. Keep the data model that best represents the problem, and let each mark consume the rows it needs.\n\n## Field channels\n\nUse a field name when the value already exists:\n\n```ts\nlineY(rows, {\n  x: 'date',\n  y: 'revenue',\n  z: 'region',\n})\n```\n\nThe field list is type-filtered. For example:\n\n- A numeric `barX` length accepts numeric fields.\n- A date-based `lineY` x channel accepts a `Date` field.\n- `key` accepts string or number fields.\n- Nullable positional fields are valid when the mark defines missing-value behavior.\n\nIf TypeScript rejects a field name, do not cast it. Correct the row type, choose the intended field, or use a typed accessor.\n\n## Accessor channels\n\nUse an accessor for derived values:\n\n```ts\ndot(rows, {\n  x: (row) => row.revenue / row.accounts,\n  y: (row) => row.retained / row.accounts,\n})\n```\n\nEvery accessor receives:\n\n```ts\n;(datum, { index, data }) => value\n```\n\n`datum` has the exact source type. The context contains the zero-based `index`\nand readonly materialized `data` array. Accessors are evaluated when the mark\ninitializes; keep expensive cross-row transforms in application code.\n\n## Positional channels\n\nThe x and y channels feed the reserved scales with the same names:\n\n```ts\nbarX(rows, {\n  x: 'revenue',\n  y: 'region',\n})\n```\n\nUse `xScale` or `yScale` when a mark should feed another named entry in the\nchart's `scales` registry. Channel names continue to describe geometry; scale\nIDs select the mapping.\n\nPositional values are also retained in each interaction `ChartPoint`:\n\n```ts\nconst handleFocus = (point: ChartPoint<Row, number, string> | null) => {\n  if (!point) return\n  console.log(point.datum, point.xValue, point.yValue)\n}\n```\n\nNormal callbacks infer these types from the definition, so explicit `ChartPoint` annotations are usually unnecessary.\n\n`number`, `string`, and `Date` are the supported chart value types. A definition can infer a union when conditional branches intentionally use different coordinate types; narrow that union with normal TypeScript control flow.\n\n## Grouping with `z`\n\n`z` identifies a semantic series or group:\n\n```ts\nlineY(rows, {\n  x: 'date',\n  y: 'value',\n  z: 'region',\n})\n```\n\nFor connected line and area marks, an explicit `z` partitions observations\ninto independent geometry. If `z` is omitted and `color` is authored, `color`\nalso supplies that path grouping. When both are present, `z` wins for geometry\nand interaction grouping while `color` remains an independent color-scale\nvalue. Omitting `color` reuses `z` for color.\n\nBars stack their length channel by default. Use `layout: group()` when multiple\nbars must occupy sub-bands within one category. Grouping uses `z` when present,\notherwise a discrete `color` channel may infer series identity. See\n[Bars and Rankings](../examples/bars-and-rankings.md).\n\n## Color channels and constants\n\nThere are two common paths:\n\n- `color` is a semantic mapping resolved by the chart color scale.\n- `z` falls back into that mapping when no separate `color` channel is set.\n- `fill` and `stroke` are final paint overrides and bypass scale mapping for\n  that paint.\n\nThe default categorical palette is useful for quick distinctions. Use an\nexplicit ordinal scale when a category must always map to the same color across\ncharts, filters, and sessions.\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'\n\nconst segmentColor = scaleOrdinal(\n  ['Consumer', 'Enterprise', 'Public'],\n  ['#2563eb', '#f97316', '#10b981'],\n)\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    dot(rows, {\n      x: 'revenue',\n      y: 'retention',\n      z: 'segment',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: revenueScale },\n    y: { scale: retentionScale },\n  },\n\n  color: {\n    scale: segmentColor,\n    legend: colorLegend({ label: 'Segment' }),\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe lightweight ordinal scale owns the stable category mapping. [Legends and\nColor](../guides/legends-and-color.md) covers continuous color, gradients, and\napplication-wide palettes.\n\n## Radius is explicit\n\nThe `r` option on `dot` is a pixel radius unless `rScale` is supplied:\n\n```ts\ndot(rows, {\n  x: 'revenue',\n  y: 'retention',\n  r: 'accounts',\n  rScale: {\n    scale: () => scaleSqrt().range([3, 22]),\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThis direct `scaleSqrt` import belongs to `d3-scale` and requires the matching direct dependency and type package.\n\nKeeping the scale visible makes the perceptual encoding reviewable. It also avoids silently treating a business measure as pixels.\n\n## Stable identity\n\nBuilt-in marks infer identity in this order:\n\n1. An explicit `key`\n2. A unique string or number `datum.id`\n3. A unique string or number `datum.data.id`\n4. A unique mark-specific positional identity\n5. Row index\n\nBars use their categorical channel. Lines and areas use their independent\naxis. Dots and text try x, then y, then the x/y tuple. Rects and cells use\ntheir x/y interval tuple. These candidates are checked within each interaction\ngroup; a collision rejects the candidate and continues to the next fallback.\nThe nested ID convention covers rows emitted by wrappers such as D3 pie\nwithout requiring an accessor solely to unwrap `data.id`.\n\nFor common rows with a unique `id`, no key option is required:\n\n```ts\nbarX(rows, {\n  id: 'product-ranking',\n  x: 'value',\n  y: 'product',\n})\n```\n\nUse an explicit key when identity lives in another field, the inferred\npositional value can change, or the automatic candidates are not unique:\n\n```ts\nbarX(rows, {\n  x: 'value',\n  y: 'product',\n  key: 'productId',\n})\n```\n\nExplicit keys are string or number values and need to be unique within the\nmark and group. When a mark-owned positional candidate is present but\nincomplete or duplicated, the mark falls back to array position and warns once\nper mark instance in development. Marks with no positional candidate also use\nrow position after checking IDs, without adding a warning for ordinary static\ndata.\n\nThe mark `id` identifies the layer. Give conditionally rendered or reordered marks an explicit, stable `id` as well.\n\n## Missing and invalid values\n\nMarks ignore positional observations they cannot materialize.\n\n- `lineY` and `areaY` split geometry at missing or non-finite positions.\n- `dot`, bars, rectangles, rules, and text omit invalid observations.\n- A negative dot radius is invalid.\n- A null group means “ungrouped.”\n\nModel a genuinely missing observation as `null` or `undefined` in the field type. Do not replace it with zero unless zero is the correct domain value.\n\nFor lines, a gap communicates missing data:\n\n```ts\ninterface Reading {\n  id: string\n  time: Date\n  temperature: number | null\n}\n\nlineY(readings, {\n  x: 'time',\n  y: 'temperature',\n})\n```\n\n## Different marks can use different rows\n\nLayering does not force one datum union:\n\n```ts\nconst marks = [\n  rect(maintenanceWindows, {\n    x1: 'start',\n    x2: 'end',\n    y1: 'minimum',\n    y2: 'maximum',\n  }),\n  lineY(readings, {\n    x: 'time',\n    y: 'temperature',\n  }),\n  text(annotations, {\n    x: 'time',\n    y: 'value',\n    text: 'label',\n  }),\n]\n```\n\nThe definition’s interaction datum becomes the honest union of point-emitting mark data. Callbacks narrow that union using your existing discriminants or type guards.\n\n## Derived data stays explicit\n\nGrouping, binning, rolling, normalization, selection, and reusable stack\nendpoints happen before mark construction. Use the pure transforms from\nTanStack Charts or an ordinary application function:\n\n```ts\nconst bins = binX(observations, {\n  value: 'latency',\n  thresholds: 24,\n})\n```\n\nTransforms return materialized typed rows and retain source lineage. They do\nnot rewrite mark options or own reactivity. Run them beside `defineChart` or\ninside the framework primitive that memoizes the definition. The resulting\nrows flow into ordinary marks.\n\n[Transforms and Reactivity](../guides/transforms-and-reactivity.md) shows the\ncomplete raw-data-to-mark path and separates application memoization from\nresponsive layout work.\n\n## Complete bubble-scatter example\n\n```ts group=bubble-scatter env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { scaleSqrt } from 'd3-scale'\nimport { colorLegend, defineChart, dot } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'\nimport { penguins, type PenguinsRow } from './data'\n\ntype CompletePenguin = PenguinsRow & {\n  culmen_length_mm: number\n  culmen_depth_mm: number\n  body_mass_g: number\n}\n\nconst rows = penguins.filter(\n  (row): row is CompletePenguin =>\n    row.culmen_length_mm !== null &&\n    row.culmen_depth_mm !== null &&\n    row.body_mass_g !== null,\n)\nconst species = ['Adelie', 'Chinstrap', 'Gentoo']\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    dot(rows, {\n      x: 'culmen_length_mm',\n      y: 'culmen_depth_mm',\n      color: 'species',\n      r: 'body_mass_g',\n      rScale: {\n        scale: () => scaleSqrt().range([3, 11]),\n      },\n      fillOpacity: 0.78,\n      stroke: 'currentColor',\n      strokeOpacity: 0.28,\n      strokeWidth: 0.75,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Bill length (mm)' },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Bill depth (mm)' },\n    },\n  },\n\n  color: {\n    scale: scaleOrdinal(species, ['#2563eb', '#f97316', '#10b981']),\n    legend: colorLegend({ label: 'Species' }),\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=bubble-scatter file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport interface PenguinsRow {\n  species: string\n  culmen_length_mm: number | null\n  culmen_depth_mm: number | null\n  body_mass_g: number | null\n}\n\nexport const penguins: readonly PenguinsRow[] = [\n  {\n    species: 'Adelie',\n    culmen_length_mm: 39.1,\n    culmen_depth_mm: 18.7,\n    body_mass_g: 3750,\n  },\n  {\n    species: 'Adelie',\n    culmen_length_mm: 40.3,\n    culmen_depth_mm: 18,\n    body_mass_g: 3250,\n  },\n  {\n    species: 'Chinstrap',\n    culmen_length_mm: 46.5,\n    culmen_depth_mm: 17.9,\n    body_mass_g: 3500,\n  },\n  {\n    species: 'Chinstrap',\n    culmen_length_mm: 50,\n    culmen_depth_mm: 19.5,\n    body_mass_g: 3900,\n  },\n  {\n    species: 'Gentoo',\n    culmen_length_mm: 46.1,\n    culmen_depth_mm: 13.2,\n    body_mass_g: 4500,\n  },\n  {\n    species: 'Gentoo',\n    culmen_length_mm: 50,\n    culmen_depth_mm: 16.3,\n    body_mass_g: 5700,\n  },\n  {\n    species: 'Gentoo',\n    culmen_length_mm: null,\n    culmen_depth_mm: null,\n    body_mass_g: null,\n  },\n]\n```\n\nThe filter only removes observations missing a plotted measurement; the marks\nstill use the source dataset's field names. The axes and categorical color use\nlightweight scales. Bubble area needs the nonlinear D3 `scaleSqrt`, so install\n`d3-scale` and `@types/d3-scale` for that one mapping.\n\nFor every built-in channel, see the relevant [Mark Reference](../reference/marks/line-and-area.md). For inference rules and custom datum unions, see [TypeScript](../guides/typescript.md).\n"}
